---
title: Ross Breadmore
layout: home.njk
---

<div class="hero">
  <h1 class="hero-title">Ross<br>Bread<br>More</h1>
  <p class="hero-sub">Design and product leader. Executive Director of UX at JPMorgan Chase. Former CPO at 4G Capital. Builder of teams, products, and better institutions.</p>
</div>

<section class="skills">
  <p class="skills-label">20 years · 7 lessons</p>

  <div class="timeline">

    <div class="timeline-item" data-side="right">
      <div class="timeline-node"><span>01</span></div>
      <div class="timeline-body">
        <button class="timeline-trigger">
          <span class="skill-name">Visual Thinking</span>
          <span class="skill-company">Falmouth University</span>
        </button>
        <div class="timeline-detail">
          <p>A degree in illustration taught me more than any business school could. Learning to see problems, to make abstract ideas concrete, and to answer a brief with something unexpected — that instinct has never left me. It's just applied to systems and organisations now, rather than paper.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="timeline-item" data-side="left">
      <div class="timeline-node"><span>02</span></div>
      <div class="timeline-body">
        <button class="timeline-trigger">
          <span class="skill-name">Working in the Open</span>
          <span class="skill-company">NixonMcInnes</span>
        </button>
        <div class="timeline-detail">
          <p>A small agency with a big philosophy. I learned that transparency isn't a risk — it's a strategy. Sharing work early, inviting critique, building in public. Everything I do now starts with that same open hand.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="timeline-item" data-side="right">
      <div class="timeline-node"><span>03</span></div>
      <div class="timeline-body">
        <button class="timeline-trigger">
          <span class="skill-name">Shoulder to Shoulder</span>
          <span class="skill-company">We Are Friday</span>
        </button>
        <div class="timeline-detail">
          <p>Embedded in engineering teams before "cross-functional" became a buzzword. I learned that the best products come from designers and engineers working side by side, not throwing specs over a wall. Agile isn't a process — it's a posture.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="timeline-item" data-side="left">
      <div class="timeline-node"><span>04</span></div>
      <div class="timeline-body">
        <button class="timeline-trigger">
          <span class="skill-name">Storytelling</span>
          <span class="skill-company">Bold Rocket</span>
        </button>
        <div class="timeline-detail">
          <p>How to turn an idea into a narrative that moves people. Energy, conviction, and knowing how to read a room. The work that doesn't get told doesn't get done — and telling it well is a craft worth taking seriously.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="timeline-item" data-side="right">
      <div class="timeline-node"><span>05</span></div>
      <div class="timeline-body">
        <button class="timeline-trigger">
          <span class="skill-name">Making it Simple</span>
          <span class="skill-company">Lloyds Banking Group</span>
        </button>
        <div class="timeline-detail">
          <p>Five years inside one of the UK's biggest institutions taught me that simplicity is hard. It requires patience, institutional knowledge, and the courage to cut. The simple version is almost always the harder version to make — but it's the one that works.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="timeline-item" data-side="left">
      <div class="timeline-node"><span>06</span></div>
      <div class="timeline-body">
        <button class="timeline-trigger">
          <span class="skill-name">Productive Tension</span>
          <span class="skill-company">4G Capital</span>
        </button>
        <div class="timeline-detail">
          <p>Good product leadership isn't comfortable. It requires holding the tension between what the business wants and what users need — and not letting either side win too easily. Working in East Africa sharpened this further: the stakes were real, the constraints were real, and the easy answer was rarely the right one.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="timeline-item" data-side="right">
      <div class="timeline-node"><span>07</span></div>
      <div class="timeline-body">
        <button class="timeline-trigger">
          <span class="skill-name">Disruption with Purpose</span>
          <span class="skill-company">JPMorgan Chase</span>
        </button>
        <div class="timeline-detail">
          <p>Bringing all of the above into one of the world's largest financial institutions. The goal isn't to break things — it's to change them from the inside, in ways that actually stick. Twenty years of unconventional moves, finally making sense.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

  </div>
</section>

<ul class="post-list">
  {% for post in collections.posts %}
    <li>
      <a href="{{ post.url }}">
        <span class="post-list-title">{{ post.data.title }}</span>
        <span class="post-list-date">{{ post.date | date("MMM yyyy") }}</span>
      </a>
    </li>
  {% endfor %}
</ul>

<script>
  var items = document.querySelectorAll('.timeline-item');

  items.forEach(function(item) {
    var trigger = item.querySelector('.timeline-trigger');
    var detail = item.querySelector('.timeline-detail');

    trigger.addEventListener('click', function() {
      var isOpen = item.classList.contains('is-open');

      // Close all
      items.forEach(function(i) {
        i.classList.remove('is-open', 'is-dimmed');
      });

      if (!isOpen) {
        item.classList.add('is-open');
        items.forEach(function(i) {
          if (i !== item) i.classList.add('is-dimmed');
        });
      }
    });
  });
</script>
